# Backend, auth, and security

## Authentication vs authorization

- Use the existing NextAuth v4 route and Prisma models in [prisma/schema.prisma](prisma/schema.prisma). The route handler may be incomplete; do not assume auth works until verified in code.
- **Authentication** identifies the user; **authorization** decides what they may do.
- There is **no admin role** on `User` today. Authenticated ≠ administrator.
- Protect every administrative mutation and restricted server entry point with **server-side** authorization. UI hiding is not access control.
- Do not expose unpublished items, admin-only data, or internal errors to public clients.

## Validation and data access

- Validate untrusted input with **Zod** on the server. Client validation is UX only.
- Use **Prisma** for database access; use transactions and DB constraints when consistency requires them.
- Return safe, intentional error messages to clients; do not leak stack traces, SQL, or secrets.

## Schema and migrations

- [prisma/schema.prisma](prisma/schema.prisma) (NextAuth models) **does not match** [prisma/migrations/20261022132459_init/migration.sql](prisma/migrations/20261022132459_init/migration.sql) (legacy User/Post). Read both before any migration work.
- Do not run `migrate`, `db push`, `migrate reset`, or other destructive DB commands unless the user explicitly asks.

## File uploads (when implemented)

- Restrict to authorized users on the server.
- Enforce size limits and allowed types; validate file **content**, not only filename or client MIME.
- Use safe storage keys; prevent unintended public access to private files.
- Keep DB references and stored objects in sync on replace/delete; do not remove files still referenced elsewhere.
- Do not add cloud storage SDKs or image pipelines without user approval.

## Maintainability

Prefer focused changes over broad refactors. No unnecessary repository/DI layers for this small app.
